WEBVTT

1
00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:32.710
Listen to me, please.

2
00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:36.760
You're like me, a Homo sapiens.

3
00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:39.180
A wise human.

4
00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:42.310
Life, a miracle in the universe,

5
00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:45.600
appeared around
four billion years ago,

6
00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:49.600
and we humans
only 200,000 years ago.

7
00:01:49.690 --> 00:01:52.520
Yet we have succeeded
in disrupting the balance...

8
00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:55.780
that is so essential to life on Earth.

9
00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:59.780
Listen carefully to this
extraordinary story, which is yours,

10
00:01:59.870 --> 00:02:03.030
and decide what
you want to do with it.

11
00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:33.270
These are traces of our origins.

12
00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:37.530
At the beginning, our planet
was no more than a chaos of fire,

13
00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:40.990
formed in the wake
of its star, the sun.

14
00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:43.910
A cloud of agglutinated
dust particles,

15
00:02:43.990 --> 00:02:48.500
similar to so many
similar clusters in the universe.

16
00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:51.830
Yet this was where
the miracle of life occurred.

17
00:03:56.360 --> 00:03:59.150
Today, life- our life-

18
00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:02.700
is just a link in a chain of
innumerable living beings...

19
00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:08.160
that have succeeded one another
on Earth over nearly four billion years.

20
00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:13.580
And even today, new volcanoes
continue to sculpt our landscapes.

21
00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:17.540
They offer a glimpse of what
our Earth was like at its birth—

22
00:04:17.630 --> 00:04:20.250
molten rock surging from the depths,

23
00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:22.590
solidifying, cracking,

24
00:04:22.670 --> 00:04:25.590
blistering or spreading
in a thin crust,

25
00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:28.350
before falling dormant for a time.

26
00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:46.280
These wreaths of smoke curling
from the bowels of the Earth...

27
00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:49.780
bear witness to the Earth's
original atmosphere.

28
00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:52.790
An atmosphere devoid of oxygen.

29
00:04:54.250 --> 00:04:57.000
A dense atmosphere,
thick with water vapor,

30
00:04:57.080 --> 00:04:59.540
full of carbon dioxide.

31
00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:05.420
A furnace.

32
00:05:15.730 --> 00:05:20.980
But the Earth had an exceptional
future, offered to it by water.

33
00:05:21.070 --> 00:05:25.490
At the right distance from the sun—
not too far, not too near—

34
00:05:25.570 --> 00:05:29.620
the Earth was able to
conserve water in liquid form.

35
00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:36.580
Water vapor condensedand fell in
torrential downpours on Earth,

36
00:05:36.670 --> 00:05:38.670
and rivers appeared.

37
00:05:49.930 --> 00:05:52.640
The rivers shaped
the surface of the Earth,

38
00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:54.850
cutting their channels,

39
00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:56.980
furrowing out valleys.

40
00:05:57.060 --> 00:06:00.980
They ran toward the lowest places
on the globe to form the oceans.

41
00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:05.360
They tore minerals from the rocks,

42
00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:08.070
and gradually the freshwater
of the oceans...

43
00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:10.320
became heavy with salt.

44
00:06:18.710 --> 00:06:21.170
Water is a vital liquid.

45
00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:24.170
It irrigated these sterile expanses.

46
00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:27.380
The paths it traced
are like the veins of a body,

47
00:06:27.470 --> 00:06:32.300
the branches of a tree, the vessels
of the sap that it brought to the Earth.

48
00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:13.930
Nearly four billion years later,

49
00:07:14.010 --> 00:07:17.350
somewhere on Earth
can still be found these works of art,

50
00:07:17.430 --> 00:07:22.520
left by the volcanoes' ash, mixed
with water from Iceland's glaciers.

51
00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:42.120
There they are- matter and water,

52
00:07:42.210 --> 00:07:44.170
water and matter-

53
00:07:44.250 --> 00:07:50.170
soft and hard combined, the crucial alliance
shared by every life-form on our planet.

54
00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:09.440
Minerals and metals
are even older than the Earth.

55
00:08:09.530 --> 00:08:11.530
They are stardust.

56
00:08:11.610 --> 00:08:13.860
They provide the Earth's colors.

57
00:08:21.250 --> 00:08:25.420
Red from iron, black from carbon,

58
00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:29.090
blue from copper, yellow from sulfur.

59
00:08:55.110 --> 00:08:57.280
Where do we come from?

60
00:08:57.370 --> 00:09:00.030
Where did life first spark into being?

61
00:09:01.830 --> 00:09:03.750
A miracle of time,

62
00:09:03.830 --> 00:09:07.960
primitive life-forms still exist
in the globe's hot springs.

63
00:09:09.170 --> 00:09:11.750
They give them their colors.

64
00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:14.300
They're called archaeobacteria.

65
00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:23.220
They all feed off the Earth's heat-

66
00:09:23.310 --> 00:09:28.310
all except the cyanobacteria,
or blue-green algae.

67
00:09:31.730 --> 00:09:36.780
They alone have the capacity to turn
to the sun to capture its energy.

68
00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:46.830
They are a vital ancestor of all
yesterday's and today's plant species.

69
00:09:48.290 --> 00:09:50.380
These tiny bacteria...

70
00:09:50.460 --> 00:09:52.670
and their billions of descendants...

71
00:09:52.750 --> 00:09:56.090
changed the destiny of our planet.

72
00:09:56.170 --> 00:09:59.010
They transformed its atmosphere.

73
00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:08.270
What happened to the carbon
that poisoned the atmosphere?

74
00:10:08.350 --> 00:10:12.520
It's still here,
imprisoned in the Earth's crust.

75
00:10:12.610 --> 00:10:14.900
We can read this chapter
of the Earth's history...

76
00:10:14.990 --> 00:10:18.780
nowhere better than on the walls
of Colorado's Grand Canyon.

77
00:10:18.860 --> 00:10:22.700
They reveal nearly two billion years
of the Earth's history.

78
00:10:22.780 --> 00:10:27.710
Once upon a time, the Grand Canyon
was a sea inhabited by microorganisms.

79
00:10:27.790 --> 00:10:32.290
They grew their shells by tapping into
carbon from the atmosphere...

80
00:10:32.380 --> 00:10:34.630
dissolved in the ocean.

81
00:10:34.710 --> 00:10:37.550
When they died, the shells sank...

82
00:10:37.630 --> 00:10:40.220
and accumulated on the seabed.

83
00:10:41.850 --> 00:10:46.640
These strata are the product of those
billions and billions of shells.

84
00:10:55.650 --> 00:10:59.610
Thanks to them, the carbon
drained from the atmosphere,

85
00:10:59.700 --> 00:11:02.070
and other life-forms could develop.

86
00:11:16.420 --> 00:11:19.590
It is life that altered the atmosphere.

87
00:11:19.670 --> 00:11:23.050
Plant life fed off the sun's energy,

88
00:11:23.140 --> 00:11:25.720
which enabled it to break apart
the water molecule...

89
00:11:25.810 --> 00:11:27.720
and take the oxygen.

90
00:11:27.810 --> 00:11:30.680
And oxygen filled the air.

91
00:11:41.700 --> 00:11:45.570
The Earth's water cycle
is a process of constant renewal.

92
00:11:45.660 --> 00:11:48.620
Waterfalls, water vapor,

93
00:11:48.700 --> 00:11:52.290
clouds, rain, springs,

94
00:11:52.370 --> 00:11:57.630
rivers, seas, oceans, glaciers.

95
00:11:57.710 --> 00:11:59.710
The cycle is never broken.

96
00:11:59.800 --> 00:12:03.380
There's always the same
quantity of water on Earth.

97
00:12:03.470 --> 00:12:08.560
All the successive species on Earth
have drunk the same water.

98
00:12:09.810 --> 00:12:12.890
The astonishing matter that is water.

99
00:12:12.980 --> 00:12:15.190
One of the most unstable of all.

100
00:12:15.270 --> 00:12:17.770
It takes a liquid form
as running water,

101
00:12:17.860 --> 00:12:19.780
gaseous as vapor...

102
00:12:19.860 --> 00:12:22.530
or solid as ice.

103
00:12:30.910 --> 00:12:35.080
In Siberia, the frozen surfaces
of the lakes in winter...

104
00:12:35.170 --> 00:12:40.710
contain the traces of the forces
that water deploys when it freezes.

105
00:12:40.800 --> 00:12:43.220
Lighter than water, the ice floats,

106
00:12:43.300 --> 00:12:45.430
rather than sinking to the bottom.

107
00:12:45.510 --> 00:12:48.680
It forms a protective mantle
against the cold,

108
00:12:48.760 --> 00:12:51.010
under which life can go on.

109
00:13:33.850 --> 00:13:37.140
The engine of life is linkage.

110
00:13:37.230 --> 00:13:39.310
Everything is linked.

111
00:13:39.400 --> 00:13:41.860
Nothing is self-sufficient.

112
00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:44.150
Water and air are inseparable,

113
00:13:44.240 --> 00:13:48.160
united in life
and for our life on Earth.

114
00:13:53.240 --> 00:13:58.790
Thus, clouds form over the oceans
and bring rain to the landmasses,

115
00:13:58.880 --> 00:14:02.000
whose rivers carry water
back to the oceans.

116
00:14:02.090 --> 00:14:04.460
Sharing is everything.

117
00:14:12.850 --> 00:14:18.690
The green expanse peeking through
the clouds is the source of oxygen in the air.

118
00:14:18.770 --> 00:14:23.860
Seventy percent of this gas, without
which our lungs cannot function,

119
00:14:23.940 --> 00:14:28.240
comes from the algae
that tint the surface of the oceans.

120
00:14:29.910 --> 00:14:32.870
Our Earth relies on a balance...

121
00:14:32.950 --> 00:14:35.700
in which every being
has a role to play...

122
00:14:35.790 --> 00:14:39.750
and exists only through
the existence of another being.

123
00:14:39.830 --> 00:14:45.710
A subtle, fragile harmony
that is easily shattered.

124
00:14:45.800 --> 00:14:51.010
Thus, corals are born
from the marriage of algae and shells.

125
00:14:51.090 --> 00:14:54.720
The Great Barrier Reef,
off the coast of Australia,

126
00:14:54.810 --> 00:14:58.850
stretches over
350,000 square kilometers...

127
00:14:58.940 --> 00:15:02.730
and is home to 1,500 species of fish,

128
00:15:02.810 --> 00:15:05.230
4,000 species of mollusks...

129
00:15:05.320 --> 00:15:08.400
and 400 species of coral.

130
00:15:08.490 --> 00:15:13.120
The equilibrium of every ocean
depends on these corals.

131
00:15:34.470 --> 00:15:38.890
The Earth counts time
in billions of years.

132
00:15:38.980 --> 00:15:43.100
It took more than four billion years
for it to make trees.

133
00:15:46.610 --> 00:15:48.650
In the chain of species,

134
00:15:48.740 --> 00:15:50.780
trees are a pinnacle,

135
00:15:50.860 --> 00:15:53.950
a perfect living sculpture.

136
00:15:54.030 --> 00:15:56.070
Trees defy gravity.

137
00:15:56.160 --> 00:16:02.000
They are the only natural element
in perpetual movement toward the sky.

138
00:16:02.080 --> 00:16:07.040
They grow unhurriedly toward the sun
that nourishes their foliage.

139
00:16:27.730 --> 00:16:31.690
They have inherited from those
minuscule cyanobacteria...

140
00:16:31.780 --> 00:16:35.030
the power to capture light's energy.

141
00:16:35.110 --> 00:16:38.240
They store it and feed off it,

142
00:16:38.330 --> 00:16:40.950
turning it into wood and leaves,

143
00:16:41.040 --> 00:16:44.330
which then decompose
into a mixture of water,

144
00:16:44.420 --> 00:16:47.920
mineral, vegetable and living matter.

145
00:16:48.960 --> 00:16:51.000
And so, gradually,

146
00:16:51.090 --> 00:16:55.760
the soils that are indispensable
to life are formed.

147
00:17:04.060 --> 00:17:07.270
Soils are the factory of biodiversity.

148
00:17:08.610 --> 00:17:11.070
They are a world
of incessant activity...

149
00:17:11.150 --> 00:17:16.200
where microorganisms feed,
dig, aerate and transform.

150
00:17:16.280 --> 00:17:21.950
They make the humus, the fertile layer
to which all life on land is linked.

151
00:17:41.640 --> 00:17:44.770
What do we know about life on Earth?

152
00:17:44.850 --> 00:17:47.100
How many species are we aware of?

153
00:17:47.190 --> 00:17:50.770
A 10th of them? A hundredth perhaps?

154
00:17:50.860 --> 00:17:53.900
What do we know about
the bonds that link them?

155
00:17:55.900 --> 00:17:58.280
The Earth is a miracle.

156
00:17:58.360 --> 00:18:00.410
Life remains a mystery.

157
00:18:20.180 --> 00:18:22.100
Families of animals form,

158
00:18:22.180 --> 00:18:26.270
united by customs and rituals
that survive today.

159
00:18:47.330 --> 00:18:50.620
Some adapt to the nature
of their pasture,

160
00:18:50.710 --> 00:18:53.460
and their pasture adapts to them.

161
00:18:53.540 --> 00:18:55.500
And both gain.

162
00:18:55.590 --> 00:18:59.800
The animal sates its hunger,
and the tree can blossom again.

163
00:19:41.930 --> 00:19:44.640
In the great adventure
of life on Earth,

164
00:19:44.720 --> 00:19:46.930
every species has a role to play,

165
00:19:47.010 --> 00:19:49.560
every species has its place.

166
00:19:50.810 --> 00:19:53.600
None is futile or harmful.

167
00:19:53.690 --> 00:19:56.520
They all balance out.

168
00:20:11.160 --> 00:20:13.210
And that's where you,

169
00:20:13.290 --> 00:20:16.210
Homo sapiens- "wise human"-

170
00:20:16.290 --> 00:20:18.340
enter the story.

171
00:20:19.460 --> 00:20:22.170
You benefit from a fabulous...

172
00:20:22.260 --> 00:20:26.970
four-billion-year-old legacy
bequeathed by the Earth.

173
00:20:33.480 --> 00:20:36.270
You're only 200,000 years old,

174
00:20:36.360 --> 00:20:39.270
but you have changed
the face of the world.

175
00:20:41.280 --> 00:20:43.280
Despite your vulnerability,

176
00:20:43.360 --> 00:20:46.240
you have taken possession
of every habitat...

177
00:20:46.320 --> 00:20:49.120
and conquered swaths of territory...

178
00:20:49.200 --> 00:20:51.870
like no other species before you.

179
00:21:04.550 --> 00:21:07.680
After 180,000 nomadic years,

180
00:21:07.760 --> 00:21:11.640
and thanks to a more clement climate,
humans settled down.

181
00:21:11.720 --> 00:21:15.730
They no longer depended on
hunting for survival.

182
00:21:15.810 --> 00:21:17.900
They chose to live
in wet environments...

183
00:21:17.980 --> 00:21:22.230
that abounded
in fish, game and wild plants.

184
00:21:22.320 --> 00:21:25.900
There, where land,
water and life combine.

185
00:21:35.040 --> 00:21:38.420
Human genius inspired them
to build canoes,

186
00:21:39.540 --> 00:21:43.050
an invention that
opened up new horizons...

187
00:21:43.130 --> 00:21:46.380
and turned humans into navigators.

188
00:22:17.920 --> 00:22:21.420
Even today,
the majority of humankind...

189
00:22:21.500 --> 00:22:23.420
lives on the continents' coastlines...

190
00:22:23.500 --> 00:22:26.170
or the banks of rivers and lakes.

191
00:23:00.920 --> 00:23:05.840
The first towns grew up
less than 600 years ago.

192
00:23:05.920 --> 00:23:09.720
It was a considerable leap
in human history.

193
00:23:09.800 --> 00:23:14.890
Why towns ? Because they
allowed humans to defend themselves more easily.

194
00:23:14.970 --> 00:23:17.020
They became social beings,

195
00:23:17.100 --> 00:23:19.730
meeting and sharing
knowledge and crafts,

196
00:23:19.810 --> 00:23:22.600
blending their similarities
and differences.

197
00:23:22.690 --> 00:23:26.110
In a word, they became civilized.

198
00:24:10.440 --> 00:24:14.660
But the only energy at their disposal
was provided by nature...

199
00:24:14.740 --> 00:24:17.450
and the strength of their bodies.

200
00:24:17.540 --> 00:24:21.620
It was the story of humankind
for thousands of years.

201
00:24:21.710 --> 00:24:25.170
It still is for one person in four-

202
00:24:25.250 --> 00:24:28.290
over one and a half billion
human beings-

203
00:24:28.380 --> 00:24:32.970
more than the combined
population of all the wealthy nations.

204
00:24:47.570 --> 00:24:51.280
Taking from the Earth
only the strictly necessary.

205
00:24:51.360 --> 00:24:55.360
For a long time, the relationship
between humans and the planet...

206
00:24:55.450 --> 00:24:57.490
was evenly balanced.

207
00:24:57.580 --> 00:25:03.250
For a long time, the economy
seemed like a natural and equitable alliance.

208
00:25:18.220 --> 00:25:23.520
But life expectancy is short,
and hard labor takes its toll.

209
00:25:23.600 --> 00:25:28.020
The uncertainties of nature
weigh on daily life.

210
00:25:28.110 --> 00:25:31.650
Education is a rare privilege.

211
00:25:31.730 --> 00:25:35.150
Children are a family's only asset,

212
00:25:35.240 --> 00:25:37.490
as long as every
extra pair of hands...

213
00:25:37.570 --> 00:25:41.240
is a necessary contribution
to its subsistence.

214
00:25:51.710 --> 00:25:55.220
The Earth feeds people,
clothes them...

215
00:25:55.300 --> 00:25:57.220
and provides for their daily needs.

216
00:25:57.300 --> 00:26:00.180
Everything comes from the Earth.

217
00:26:08.310 --> 00:26:12.820
Towns change humanity's nature,
as well as its destiny.

218
00:26:12.900 --> 00:26:16.650
The farmer becomes a craftsman,
trader or peddler.

219
00:26:21.450 --> 00:26:27.250
What the Earth gives the farmer,
the city dweller buys, sells or barters.

220
00:26:27.330 --> 00:26:29.540
Goods change hands,

221
00:26:29.630 --> 00:26:31.630
along with ideas.

222
00:26:46.980 --> 00:26:51.940
Humanity's genius is to have always
had a sense of its weakness.

223
00:26:52.020 --> 00:26:55.480
Humans tried to extend
the frontiers of their territory,

224
00:26:55.570 --> 00:26:57.530
but they knew their limits.

225
00:26:57.610 --> 00:27:02.160
The physical energy and strength with which
nature had not endowed them...

226
00:27:02.240 --> 00:27:05.910
was found in the animals
they domesticated to serve them.

227
00:27:23.100 --> 00:27:27.680
But how can you conquer
the world on an empty stomach?

228
00:27:27.770 --> 00:27:30.020
The invention of agriculture...

229
00:27:30.100 --> 00:27:34.650
transformed the future of the wild
animals scavenging for food...

230
00:27:34.730 --> 00:27:36.690
that were humankind.

231
00:27:37.900 --> 00:27:41.990
Agriculture turned
their history on end.

232
00:27:42.070 --> 00:27:46.080
Agriculture was
their first great revolution.

233
00:27:46.160 --> 00:27:49.500
Developed barely
8,000 to 10,000 years ago,

234
00:27:49.580 --> 00:27:52.420
it changed
their relationship to nature.

235
00:27:59.760 --> 00:28:03.680
It brought an end to the uncertainty
of hunting and gathering.

236
00:28:03.760 --> 00:28:06.760
It resulted in the first surpluses...

237
00:28:06.850 --> 00:28:09.560
and gave birth to cities
and civilizations.

238
00:28:15.020 --> 00:28:17.150
For their agriculture,

239
00:28:17.230 --> 00:28:20.990
humans harnessed the energy
of animal species and plant life,

240
00:28:21.070 --> 00:28:24.070
from which they at last
extracted the profits.

241
00:28:24.160 --> 00:28:29.040
The memory of thousands of years
scrabbling for food faded.

242
00:28:29.120 --> 00:28:32.500
They learned to adapt the grains
that are the yeast of life...

243
00:28:32.580 --> 00:28:35.500
to different soils and climates.

244
00:28:35.580 --> 00:28:40.510
They learned to increase the yield
and multiply the number of varieties.

245
00:28:49.720 --> 00:28:51.770
Like every species on Earth,

246
00:28:51.850 --> 00:28:54.890
the principal
daily concern of all humans...

247
00:28:54.980 --> 00:28:58.860
is to feed themselves
and their family.

248
00:28:58.940 --> 00:29:02.900
When the soil is less generous
and water becomes scarce,

249
00:29:02.990 --> 00:29:07.910
humans deploy prodigious efforts
to mark a few arid acres...

250
00:29:07.990 --> 00:29:10.700
with the imprint of their labor.

251
00:29:29.010 --> 00:29:33.390
Humans shaped the land with the
patience and devotion that the Earth demands,

252
00:29:33.480 --> 00:29:38.020
in an almost sacrificial ritual
performed over and over.

253
00:30:02.210 --> 00:30:07.010
Agriculture is still the world's
most widespread occupation.

254
00:30:07.090 --> 00:30:11.640
Half of humankind tills the soil,

255
00:30:11.720 --> 00:30:15.270
over three-quarters of them by hand.

256
00:30:15.350 --> 00:30:18.900
Agriculture is like
a tradition handed down...

257
00:30:18.980 --> 00:30:23.610
from generation to generation
in sweat, graft and toil,

258
00:30:23.690 --> 00:30:28.110
because for humanity it is
a prerequisite of survival.

259
00:30:28.280 --> 00:30:31.740
But after relying on
muscle power for so long,

260
00:30:31.830 --> 00:30:36.910
humankind found a way to tap into
the energy buried deep in the Earth.

261
00:30:40.670 --> 00:30:43.130
These flames are also from plants.

262
00:30:43.210 --> 00:30:45.130
A pocket of sunlight.

263
00:30:45.210 --> 00:30:47.920
Pure energy— the energy of the sun—

264
00:30:48.010 --> 00:30:51.300
captured over millions of years
by millions of plants...

265
00:30:51.390 --> 00:30:54.010
more than a hundred million years ago.

266
00:30:54.100 --> 00:30:56.770
It's coal. It's gas.

267
00:30:56.850 --> 00:30:59.520
And above all, it's oil.

268
00:31:29.090 --> 00:31:34.300
And this pocket of sunlight freed
humans from their toil on the land.

269
00:31:38.060 --> 00:31:43.850
With oil began the era of humans
who break free of the shackles of time.

270
00:31:43.940 --> 00:31:48.150
With oil, some of us acquired
unprecedented comforts.

271
00:31:48.240 --> 00:31:50.900
And in 50 years, in a single lifetime,

272
00:31:50.990 --> 00:31:53.280
the Earth has been
more radically changed...

273
00:31:53.370 --> 00:31:57.030
than by all previous
generations of humanity.

274
00:31:59.710 --> 00:32:01.620
Faster and faster.

275
00:32:01.710 --> 00:32:06.040
In the last 60 years, the Earth's
population has almost tripled,

276
00:32:06.130 --> 00:32:10.050
and over two billion people
have moved to the cities.

277
00:32:14.680 --> 00:32:17.180
Faster and faster.

278
00:32:17.260 --> 00:32:21.520
Shenzhen, in China, with its hundreds
of skyscrapers and millions of inhabitants,

279
00:32:21.600 --> 00:32:26.270
was just a small fishing village
barely 40 years ago.

280
00:32:30.530 --> 00:32:32.450
Faster and faster.

281
00:32:32.530 --> 00:32:35.950
In Shanghai,
3,000 towers and skyscrapers...

282
00:32:36.030 --> 00:32:38.740
have been built in 20 years.

283
00:32:38.830 --> 00:32:40.870
Hundreds more are under construction.

284
00:32:47.630 --> 00:32:53.510
Today, over half of the world's
seven billion inhabitants live in cities.

285
00:33:04.060 --> 00:33:07.560
New York.
The world's first megalopolis...

286
00:33:07.650 --> 00:33:10.480
is the symbol of the exploitation
of the energy...

287
00:33:10.570 --> 00:33:13.690
the Earth supplies to human genius.

288
00:33:13.780 --> 00:33:16.320
The manpower
of millions of immigrants,

289
00:33:16.410 --> 00:33:18.320
the energy of coal,

290
00:33:18.410 --> 00:33:21.540
the unbridled power of oil.

291
00:33:21.620 --> 00:33:24.370
Electricity resulted
in the invention of elevators,

292
00:33:24.460 --> 00:33:28.250
which in turn permitted
the invention of skyscrapers.

293
00:33:28.340 --> 00:33:32.960
New York ranks as the 16th-largest
economy in the world.

294
00:33:36.340 --> 00:33:41.060
America was the first
to discover, exploit and harness...

295
00:33:41.140 --> 00:33:44.980
the phenomenal revolutionary
power of black gold.

296
00:33:45.060 --> 00:33:47.440
With its help,

297
00:33:47.520 --> 00:33:52.360
a country of farmers became a
country of agricultural industrialists.

298
00:33:53.400 --> 00:33:55.570
Machines replaced men.

299
00:33:55.650 --> 00:33:58.490
A liter of oil generates
as much energy...

300
00:33:58.570 --> 00:34:02.200
as 100 pairs of hands in 24 hours,

301
00:34:02.290 --> 00:34:07.670
but worldwide only three percent
of farmers have use of a tractor.

302
00:34:07.750 --> 00:34:12.460
Nonetheless, their output
dominates the planet.

303
00:34:12.550 --> 00:34:16.590
In the United States,
only three million farmers are left.

304
00:34:16.680 --> 00:34:21.140
They produce enough grain
to feed two billion people.

305
00:34:26.810 --> 00:34:29.350
But most of that grain
is not used to feed people.

306
00:34:29.440 --> 00:34:33.150
Here, and in all other
industrialized nations,

307
00:34:33.230 --> 00:34:37.530
it's transformed
into livestock feed or biofuels.

308
00:34:45.250 --> 00:34:47.290
The pocket of sunshine's energy...

309
00:34:47.370 --> 00:34:51.790
chased away the specter of drought
that stalked farmland.

310
00:34:51.880 --> 00:34:55.050
No spring escapes
the demands of agriculture,

311
00:34:55.130 --> 00:34:59.380
which accounts for
70% of humanity's water consumption.

312
00:35:03.140 --> 00:35:06.430
In nature, everything is linked.

313
00:35:06.520 --> 00:35:09.810
The expansion of cultivated land
and single-crop farming...

314
00:35:09.900 --> 00:35:12.860
encouraged the development
of parasites.

315
00:35:12.940 --> 00:35:17.150
Pesticides, another gift of
the petrochemical revolution,

316
00:35:17.240 --> 00:35:19.450
exterminated them.

317
00:35:19.530 --> 00:35:24.200
Bad harvests and famine
became a distant memory.

318
00:35:24.280 --> 00:35:26.200
The biggest headache now...

319
00:35:26.290 --> 00:35:30.410
was what to do with the surpluses
engendered by modern agriculture.

320
00:35:41.890 --> 00:35:45.220
But toxic pesticides
seeped into the air,

321
00:35:45.310 --> 00:35:48.810
soil, plants, animals,
rivers and oceans.

322
00:35:48.890 --> 00:35:51.270
They penetrated the heart of cells...

323
00:35:51.350 --> 00:35:56.480
similar to the mother cell
that is shared by all forms of life.

324
00:35:56.570 --> 00:36:00.070
Are they harmful to the humans
that they released from hunger?

325
00:36:00.150 --> 00:36:03.990
These farmers,
in their yellow protective suits,

326
00:36:04.070 --> 00:36:06.280
probably have a good idea.

327
00:36:21.420 --> 00:36:26.140
The new agriculture abolished
the dependence on soils and seasons.

328
00:36:31.270 --> 00:36:33.980
Fertilizers produced
unprecedented results...

329
00:36:34.060 --> 00:36:37.650
on plots of land thus far ignored.

330
00:36:37.730 --> 00:36:40.570
Crops adapted to soils and climates...

331
00:36:40.650 --> 00:36:45.280
gave way to the most productive
varieties and the easiest to transport.

332
00:36:45.370 --> 00:36:47.370
And so, in the last century,

333
00:36:47.450 --> 00:36:52.370
three-quarters of the varieties developed
by farmers over thousands of years...

334
00:36:52.460 --> 00:36:55.710
have been wiped out.

335
00:36:55.790 --> 00:37:00.500
As far as the eye can see,
fertilizer below, plastic on top.

336
00:37:00.590 --> 00:37:03.470
The greenhouses of Almeria in Spain...

337
00:37:03.550 --> 00:37:05.680
are Europe's vegetable garden.

338
00:37:05.760 --> 00:37:08.720
A city of uniformly sized
vegetables...

339
00:37:08.810 --> 00:37:11.720
waits every day
for the hundreds of trucks...

340
00:37:11.810 --> 00:37:14.810
that will take them
to the continent's supermarkets.

341
00:37:16.150 --> 00:37:18.060
The more a country develops,

342
00:37:18.150 --> 00:37:21.230
the more meat its inhabitants consume.

343
00:37:21.320 --> 00:37:24.570
How can growing worldwide
demand be satisfied...

344
00:37:24.650 --> 00:37:28.740
without recourse to concentration
camp-style cattle farms?

345
00:37:28.830 --> 00:37:30.740
Faster and faster.

346
00:37:30.830 --> 00:37:34.250
Like the life cycle of livestock
which may never see a meadow,

347
00:37:34.330 --> 00:37:39.290
manufacturing meat faster than
the animal has become a daily routine.

348
00:37:49.680 --> 00:37:53.720
In these vast food lots,
trampled by millions of cattle,

349
00:37:53.810 --> 00:37:56.020
not a blade of grass grows.

350
00:37:57.270 --> 00:37:59.810
A fleet of trucks from
every corner of the country...

351
00:37:59.900 --> 00:38:02.690
brings in tons of grain, soy meal...

352
00:38:02.780 --> 00:38:07.530
and protein-rich granules
that will become tons of meat.

353
00:38:07.610 --> 00:38:11.330
The result is that it takes
100 liters of water...

354
00:38:11.410 --> 00:38:14.330
to produce one kilogram of potatoes,

355
00:38:14.410 --> 00:38:17.540
4,000 for one kilo of rice...

356
00:38:17.620 --> 00:38:21.880
and 13,000 for one kilo of beef.

357
00:38:21.960 --> 00:38:25.880
Not to mention the oil guzzled in the
production process and transport.

358
00:38:35.520 --> 00:38:38.980
Our agriculture
has become oil-powered.

359
00:38:39.060 --> 00:38:41.690
It feeds twice as many
humans on Earth...

360
00:38:41.770 --> 00:38:46.650
but has replaced diversity
with standardization.

361
00:38:46.740 --> 00:38:50.320
It has offered many of us
comforts we could only dream of,

362
00:38:50.410 --> 00:38:54.870
but it makes our way of life
totally dependent on oil.

363
00:38:56.910 --> 00:39:00.370
This is the new measure of time.

364
00:39:00.460 --> 00:39:03.090
Our world's clock
now beats to the rhythm...

365
00:39:03.170 --> 00:39:05.130
of these indefatigable machines...

366
00:39:05.210 --> 00:39:08.630
tapping into the pocket of sunlight.

367
00:39:08.720 --> 00:39:11.680
Their regularity reassures us.

368
00:39:11.760 --> 00:39:15.600
The tiniest hiccup
throws us into disarray.

369
00:39:15.680 --> 00:39:19.270
The whole planet is attentive
to these metronomes...

370
00:39:19.350 --> 00:39:22.860
of our hopes and illusions.

371
00:39:22.940 --> 00:39:26.980
The same hopes and illusions
that proliferate along with our needs,

372
00:39:27.070 --> 00:39:30.860
increasingly insatiable desires
and profligacy.

373
00:39:30.950 --> 00:39:33.990
We know that the end
of cheap oil is imminent,

374
00:39:34.080 --> 00:39:36.290
but we refuse to believe it.

375
00:39:41.540 --> 00:39:46.960
For many of us, the American dream
is embodied by a legendary name:

376
00:39:47.050 --> 00:39:50.050
Los Angeles.

377
00:39:50.130 --> 00:39:53.390
In this city that stretches
over 100 kilometers,

378
00:39:53.470 --> 00:39:57.810
the number of cars is almost
equal to the number of inhabitants.

379
00:40:09.900 --> 00:40:13.320
Here, energy puts on
a fantastic show every night.

380
00:40:46.860 --> 00:40:50.900
The days seem to be no more
than the pale reflection of nights...

381
00:40:50.990 --> 00:40:53.950
that turn the city into a starry sky.

382
00:40:57.240 --> 00:40:59.830
Faster and faster.

383
00:40:59.910 --> 00:41:03.960
Distances are no longer counted
in miles but in minutes.

384
00:41:04.040 --> 00:41:07.960
The automobile shapes new suburbs
where every home is a castle,

385
00:41:08.040 --> 00:41:11.460
a safe distance from
the asphyxiated city centers,

386
00:41:11.550 --> 00:41:15.380
and where neat rows of houses
huddle round dead-end streets.

387
00:41:16.430 --> 00:41:18.760
The model of a lucky few countries...

388
00:41:18.850 --> 00:41:20.810
has become a universal dream,

389
00:41:20.890 --> 00:41:24.310
preached by televisions
all over the world.

390
00:41:28.150 --> 00:41:30.650
Even here in Beijing,

391
00:41:30.730 --> 00:41:33.360
it is cloned, copied and reproduced...

392
00:41:33.440 --> 00:41:38.410
in these formatted houses
that have wiped pagodas off the map.

393
00:41:50.920 --> 00:41:56.180
The automobile has become
the symbol of comfort and progress.

394
00:41:57.930 --> 00:42:00.720
If this model were followed
by every society,

395
00:42:00.810 --> 00:42:05.520
the planet wouldn't have 900 million
vehicles, as it does today,

396
00:42:05.600 --> 00:42:07.980
but five billion.

397
00:42:15.400 --> 00:42:17.320
Faster and faster.

398
00:42:17.410 --> 00:42:21.530
The more the world develops,
the greater its thirst for energy.

399
00:42:21.620 --> 00:42:26.250
Everywhere, machines dig,
bore and rip from the Earth...

400
00:42:26.330 --> 00:42:30.460
the pieces of stars buried in
its depths since its creation:

401
00:42:30.540 --> 00:42:32.540
minerals.

402
00:42:56.030 --> 00:43:00.240
In the next 20 years, more ore
will be extracted from the Earth...

403
00:43:00.320 --> 00:43:03.330
than in the whole
of humanity's history.

404
00:43:04.450 --> 00:43:06.620
As a privilege of power,

405
00:43:06.700 --> 00:43:08.960
80% of this mineral wealth...

406
00:43:09.040 --> 00:43:13.420
is consumed by 20%
of the world's population.

407
00:43:31.690 --> 00:43:33.860
Before the end of this century,

408
00:43:33.940 --> 00:43:38.650
excessive mining will have exhausted
nearly all the planet's reserves.

409
00:43:48.410 --> 00:43:50.460
Faster and faster.

410
00:43:50.540 --> 00:43:52.870
Shipyards churn out oil tankers,

411
00:43:52.960 --> 00:43:55.130
container ships and gas tankers...

412
00:43:55.210 --> 00:43:59.210
to cater for the demands
of globalized industrial production.

413
00:44:03.800 --> 00:44:06.890
Most consumer goods travel
thousands of kilometers...

414
00:44:06.970 --> 00:44:11.560
from the country of production
to the country of consumption.

415
00:44:11.640 --> 00:44:14.980
Since 1950,
the volume of international trade...

416
00:44:15.060 --> 00:44:17.860
has increased 20 times over.

417
00:44:28.370 --> 00:44:32.000
Ninety percent of trade goes by sea.

418
00:44:32.080 --> 00:44:37.420
500 million containers
are transported every year,

419
00:44:37.500 --> 00:44:40.510
headed for the world's
major hubs of consumption,

420
00:44:40.590 --> 00:44:42.590
such as Dubai.

421
00:44:44.050 --> 00:44:48.260
Dubai is one of the biggest
construction sites in the world—

422
00:44:48.350 --> 00:44:51.600
a country where the impossible
becomes possible.

423
00:44:51.680 --> 00:44:55.060
Building artificial islands
in the sea, for example.

424
00:45:24.680 --> 00:45:27.760
Dubai has few natural resources,

425
00:45:27.850 --> 00:45:31.970
but with the money from oil, it can bring
millions of tons of material and people...

426
00:45:32.060 --> 00:45:33.980
from all over the world.

427
00:45:34.060 --> 00:45:38.360
It can build forests of skyscrapers,
each one taller than the last,

428
00:45:38.440 --> 00:45:41.530
or even a ski slope
in the middle of the desert.

429
00:45:41.610 --> 00:45:46.110
Dubai has no farmland,
but it can import food.

430
00:45:46.200 --> 00:45:48.120
Dubai has no water,

431
00:45:48.200 --> 00:45:51.240
but it can afford to expend
immense amounts of energy...

432
00:45:51.330 --> 00:45:56.160
to desalinate seawater and build
the highest skyscrapers in the world.

433
00:45:56.250 --> 00:46:00.630
Dubai has endless sun
but no solar panels.

434
00:46:00.710 --> 00:46:03.630
It is the city of more is more,

435
00:46:03.710 --> 00:46:06.260
where the wildest dreams
become reality.

436
00:46:06.340 --> 00:46:09.680
Dubai is a sort of culmination
of the Western model,

437
00:46:09.760 --> 00:46:13.390
with its 800-meter high totem
to total modernity...

438
00:46:13.470 --> 00:46:16.180
that never fails to amaze the world.

439
00:46:16.270 --> 00:46:19.480
Excessive? Perhaps.

440
00:46:19.560 --> 00:46:22.400
Dubai appears to have made its choice.

441
00:46:22.480 --> 00:46:25.490
It is like the new beacon
for all the world's money.

442
00:46:38.460 --> 00:46:41.630
Nothing seems further removed
from nature than Dubai,

443
00:46:41.710 --> 00:46:45.710
although nothing depends
on nature more than Dubai.

444
00:46:47.550 --> 00:46:51.050
The city merely follows
the model of wealthy nations.

445
00:46:51.140 --> 00:46:55.430
We haven't understood that we're
depleting what nature provides.

446
00:47:40.310 --> 00:47:44.360
What do we know of the marine world,
of which we see only the surface,

447
00:47:44.440 --> 00:47:47.940
and which covers
three-quarters of the planet?

448
00:47:48.030 --> 00:47:50.740
The ocean depths remain a secret.

449
00:47:50.820 --> 00:47:55.950
They contain thousands of species
whose existence remains a mystery to us.

450
00:48:11.260 --> 00:48:16.180
Since 1950, fishing catches
have increased fivefold,

451
00:48:16.260 --> 00:48:20.980
from 18 to 100 million
metric tons a year.

452
00:48:21.060 --> 00:48:24.980
Thousands of factory ships
are emptying the oceans.

453
00:48:33.490 --> 00:48:36.410
Three-quarters
of fishing grounds are exhausted,

454
00:48:36.490 --> 00:48:39.620
depleted or in danger of being so.

455
00:49:19.330 --> 00:49:22.450
Most large fish have been
fished out of existence,

456
00:49:22.540 --> 00:49:25.580
since they have no time to reproduce.

457
00:49:25.670 --> 00:49:29.880
We are destroying the cycle of a life
that was given to us.

458
00:49:40.560 --> 00:49:45.890
On the coastlines, signs of the
exhaustion of stocks abound.

459
00:49:45.980 --> 00:49:49.940
First sign: Colonies of sea mammals
are getting smaller.

460
00:49:50.020 --> 00:49:53.820
Made vulnerable by urbanization
of the coasts and pollution,

461
00:49:53.900 --> 00:49:57.070
they now face a new threat: famine.

462
00:49:57.160 --> 00:50:00.660
In their unequal battle
against industrial fishing fleets,

463
00:50:00.740 --> 00:50:03.750
they can't find enough fish
to feed their young.

464
00:50:12.760 --> 00:50:14.710
Second sign:

465
00:50:14.800 --> 00:50:18.840
Seabirds must fly ever greater
distances to find food.

466
00:50:18.930 --> 00:50:24.770
At the current rate, all fish stocks
are threatened with exhaustion.

467
00:50:36.530 --> 00:50:40.700
In Dakar, traditional net fishing
boomed in the years of plenty,

468
00:50:40.780 --> 00:50:43.830
but today, fish stocks are dwindling.

469
00:51:03.720 --> 00:51:07.890
Fish is the staple diet
of one in five humans.

470
00:51:34.420 --> 00:51:38.130
Can we envision the inconceivable?

471
00:51:38.220 --> 00:51:40.380
Abandoned boats,

472
00:51:40.470 --> 00:51:42.550
seas devoid offish?

473
00:52:07.500 --> 00:52:11.370
We have forgotten
that resources are scarce.

474
00:52:11.460 --> 00:52:15.290
500 million humans live
in the world's desert lands,

475
00:52:15.380 --> 00:52:17.960
more than the combined
population of Europe.

476
00:52:18.050 --> 00:52:20.970
They know the value of water.

477
00:52:21.050 --> 00:52:23.430
They know how to use it sparingly.

478
00:52:23.510 --> 00:52:27.390
Here, they depend on wells
replenished by fossil water,

479
00:52:27.470 --> 00:52:31.980
which accumulated underground in the days
when it rained on these deserts,

480
00:52:32.060 --> 00:52:34.560
25,000 years ago.

481
00:52:41.360 --> 00:52:44.990
Fossil water also enables crops
to be grown in the desert...

482
00:52:45.070 --> 00:52:47.830
to provide food for local populations.

483
00:52:47.910 --> 00:52:49.910
The fields' circular shape...

484
00:52:50.000 --> 00:52:54.420
derives from the pipes that
irrigate them around a central pivot.

485
00:53:08.760 --> 00:53:12.230
But there is a heavy price to pay.

486
00:53:12.310 --> 00:53:15.980
Fossil water
is a nonrenewable resource.

487
00:53:23.780 --> 00:53:29.120
In Saudi Arabia, the dream of
industrial farming in the desert has faded.

488
00:53:29.200 --> 00:53:31.240
As if on a parchment map,

489
00:53:31.330 --> 00:53:35.620
the light spots on this patchwork
show abandoned plots.

490
00:53:35.710 --> 00:53:38.330
The irrigation equipment
is still there.

491
00:53:38.420 --> 00:53:40.710
The energy to pump water also.

492
00:53:40.800 --> 00:53:44.800
But the fossil water reserves
are severely depleted.

493
00:53:57.310 --> 00:54:00.770
Israel turned the desert
into arable land.

494
00:54:00.860 --> 00:54:04.530
Even though these hothouses
are now irrigated drop by drop,

495
00:54:04.610 --> 00:54:08.780
water consumption continues
to increase along with exports.

496
00:54:16.710 --> 00:54:20.420
The once mighty river Jordan
is now just a trickle.

497
00:54:20.500 --> 00:54:23.550
Its water has flown to supermarkets
all over the world...

498
00:54:23.630 --> 00:54:26.340
in crates of fruit and vegetables.

499
00:55:01.380 --> 00:55:03.590
The Jordan's fate is not unique.

500
00:55:03.670 --> 00:55:07.380
Across the planet,
one major river in 10...

501
00:55:07.470 --> 00:55:11.340
no longer flows into the sea
for several months of the year.

502
00:55:22.610 --> 00:55:26.320
The Dead Sea derives its name
from its incredibly high salinity...

503
00:55:26.400 --> 00:55:28.570
that makes all life impossible.

504
00:55:28.650 --> 00:55:30.950
Deprived of the Jordan's water,

505
00:55:31.030 --> 00:55:34.370
its level goes down
by over one meter per year.

506
00:55:34.450 --> 00:55:37.080
Its salinity is increasing.

507
00:55:37.160 --> 00:55:39.160
Evaporation, due to the heat,

508
00:55:39.250 --> 00:55:42.330
produces these fine islands
of salt evaporites-

509
00:55:42.420 --> 00:55:44.420
beautiful but sterile.

510
00:56:08.400 --> 00:56:13.070
In Rajasthan, India,
Udaipur is a miracle of water.

511
00:56:13.160 --> 00:56:17.080
The city was made possible
by a system of dams and channels...

512
00:56:17.160 --> 00:56:19.410
that created an artificial lake.

513
00:56:20.620 --> 00:56:23.670
For its architects,
was water so precious...

514
00:56:23.750 --> 00:56:26.290
that they dedicated a palace to it?

515
00:56:51.820 --> 00:56:54.490
India risks being the country
that suffers most...

516
00:56:54.570 --> 00:56:57.660
from the lack of water
in the coming century.

517
00:56:57.740 --> 00:57:01.450
Massive irrigation
has fed the growing population,

518
00:57:01.540 --> 00:57:07.420
and in the last 50 years,
21 million wells have been dug.

519
00:57:07.500 --> 00:57:10.800
The victory over famine
has a downside, however.

520
00:57:10.880 --> 00:57:16.470
In many parts of the country, the drill
has to sink ever deeper to hit water.

521
00:57:16.550 --> 00:57:19.010
In western India,

522
00:57:19.100 --> 00:57:21.680
30% of wells have been abandoned.

523
00:57:27.480 --> 00:57:30.690
The underground
aquifers are drying out.

524
00:57:50.960 --> 00:57:55.760
Vast reservoirs will catch the
monsoon rains to replenish the aquifers.

525
00:58:00.600 --> 00:58:05.480
In dry season, women from
local villages dig them with their bare hands.

526
00:58:29.920 --> 00:58:32.000
Thousands of kilometers away,

527
00:58:32.090 --> 00:58:34.010
800 to 1,000 liters of water...

528
00:58:34.090 --> 00:58:38.180
are consumed per person per day.

529
00:58:38.260 --> 00:58:41.220
Las Vegas was built out of the desert.

530
00:58:41.310 --> 00:58:43.760
Millions of people live there.

531
00:58:43.850 --> 00:58:46.680
Thousands more arrive every month.

532
00:58:46.770 --> 00:58:52.190
The inhabitants of Las Vegas are among
the biggest consumers of water in the world.

533
00:58:58.110 --> 00:59:00.030
Palm Springs is another desert city...

534
00:59:00.120 --> 00:59:03.580
with tropical vegetation
and lush golf courses.

535
00:59:21.350 --> 00:59:24.640
How long can this mirage
continue to prosper?

536
00:59:32.150 --> 00:59:34.730
The Earth cannot keep up.

537
00:59:34.820 --> 00:59:38.190
The Colorado River,
which brings water to these cities,

538
00:59:38.280 --> 00:59:41.450
is one of those rivers
that no longer reaches the sea.

539
00:59:41.530 --> 00:59:43.990
Even more alarmingly,

540
00:59:44.080 --> 00:59:47.290
its flow is diminishing at source.

541
00:59:47.370 --> 00:59:51.170
Water levels in the catchment lakes
along its course...

542
00:59:51.250 --> 00:59:54.130
are plummeting.

543
00:59:54.210 --> 00:59:58.550
Lake Powell took 17 years
to reach high-water mark.

544
00:59:58.630 --> 01:00:01.880
Its level is now half of that.

545
01:00:03.260 --> 01:00:06.430
Water shortages could affect
nearly two billion people...

546
01:00:06.520 --> 01:00:08.520
before 2025.

547
01:00:10.350 --> 01:00:13.850
Yet water is still abundant
in unspoiled regions of the planet.

548
01:00:17.360 --> 01:00:19.280
The wetlands.

549
01:00:29.540 --> 01:00:34.290
These wetlands are crucial
to all life on Earth.

550
01:00:34.380 --> 01:00:37.380
They represent
six percent of the planet.

551
01:00:39.880 --> 01:00:43.680
Marshes are sponges
that regulate the flow of water.

552
01:00:46.390 --> 01:00:48.470
They absorb it in the wet season...

553
01:00:48.560 --> 01:00:51.270
and release it in the dry season.

554
01:01:06.240 --> 01:01:08.580
The water runs off the mountain peaks,

555
01:01:08.660 --> 01:01:12.660
carrying with it the seeds
of the regions it flows through.

556
01:01:12.750 --> 01:01:15.580
This process gives birth
to unique landscapes,

557
01:01:15.670 --> 01:01:20.000
where the diversity of species
is unequaled in its richness.

558
01:01:24.590 --> 01:01:28.180
Under the calm water
lies a veritable factory...

559
01:01:28.260 --> 01:01:31.430
where this ultimately linked
richness and diversity...

560
01:01:31.520 --> 01:01:36.060
patiently filters the water
and digests all the pollution.

561
01:01:36.150 --> 01:01:38.480
Marshes are
indispensable environments...

562
01:01:38.570 --> 01:01:42.530
for the regeneration
and purification of water.

563
01:01:55.210 --> 01:01:59.210
These wetlands were always seen
as unhealthy expanses,

564
01:01:59.290 --> 01:02:02.090
unfit for human habitation.

565
01:02:02.170 --> 01:02:04.380
In our race to conquer more land,

566
01:02:04.470 --> 01:02:08.050
we have reclaimed them
as pasture for our livestock,

567
01:02:08.140 --> 01:02:11.890
or as land for agriculture
or building.

568
01:02:31.540 --> 01:02:37.040
In the last century, half of the
world's marshes were drained.

569
01:02:37.120 --> 01:02:41.540
We know neither their richness
nor their role.

570
01:02:46.680 --> 01:02:48.760
All living matter is linked.

571
01:02:51.510 --> 01:02:56.270
Water, air, soil, trees.

572
01:02:56.350 --> 01:03:00.190
The world's magic
is right in front of our eyes.

573
01:03:34.220 --> 01:03:38.770
Trees breathe groundwater
into the atmosphere as light mist.

574
01:03:38.850 --> 01:03:42.440
They form a canopy that alleviates
the impact of heavy rains...

575
01:03:42.520 --> 01:03:45.360
and protects the soil from erosion.

576
01:03:48.860 --> 01:03:52.700
The forests provide the humidity
that is necessary for life.

577
01:03:52.780 --> 01:03:56.830
They are the mother
and father of rain.

578
01:04:00.540 --> 01:04:03.830
The forests store carbon.

579
01:04:03.920 --> 01:04:07.550
They contain more than
all the Earth's atmosphere.

580
01:04:07.630 --> 01:04:10.510
They are the cornerstone
of the climatic balance...

581
01:04:10.590 --> 01:04:14.350
on which we all depend.

582
01:04:21.980 --> 01:04:27.360
Trees provide a habitat for three-quarters
of the planet's biodiversity—

583
01:04:27.440 --> 01:04:31.530
that is to say, of all life on Earth.

584
01:04:31.610 --> 01:04:36.870
Every year, we discover new species
we had no idea existed—

585
01:04:36.950 --> 01:04:40.160
insects, birds, mammals.

586
01:05:12.490 --> 01:05:16.240
These forests provide
the remedies that cure us.

587
01:05:16.320 --> 01:05:18.740
The substances secreted
by these plants...

588
01:05:18.830 --> 01:05:21.410
can be recognized by our bodies.

589
01:05:21.500 --> 01:05:24.250
Our cells talk the same language.

590
01:05:24.330 --> 01:05:27.460
We are of the same family.

591
01:05:51.190 --> 01:05:55.070
Mangroves are forests
that step out onto the sea.

592
01:05:55.160 --> 01:05:59.200
Like coral reefs,
they are a nursery for the oceans.

593
01:05:59.280 --> 01:06:02.490
Their roots entwine
and form a shelter for the fish...

594
01:06:02.580 --> 01:06:05.540
and mollusks that come to breed.

595
01:06:05.620 --> 01:06:08.040
Mangroves protect the coasts
from hurricanes,

596
01:06:08.130 --> 01:06:10.880
tidal waves and erosion by the sea.

597
01:06:10.960 --> 01:06:13.710
Whole peoples depend on them.

598
01:06:13.800 --> 01:06:19.090
Yet, they were reduced by half
during the 20th century.

599
01:06:22.810 --> 01:06:25.730
One of the reasons
for the ongoing disaster...

600
01:06:25.810 --> 01:06:30.150
is these shrimp farms installed
on the mangroves' rich waters.

601
01:06:30.230 --> 01:06:33.860
Ventilators aerate pools
full of antibiotics...

602
01:06:33.940 --> 01:06:36.570
to prevent the asphyxiation
of the shrimps,

603
01:06:36.660 --> 01:06:38.990
not that of the mangroves.

604
01:06:44.330 --> 01:06:50.000
Since the 1960s, deforestation
has constantly gathered pace.

605
01:06:50.090 --> 01:06:54.250
Every year, 13 million hectares
of tropical forest-

606
01:06:54.340 --> 01:06:56.590
an area the size of Illinois-

607
01:06:56.680 --> 01:07:00.260
disappear in smoke and as lumber.

608
01:07:07.940 --> 01:07:11.190
The world's largest rain forest,
the Amazon,

609
01:07:11.270 --> 01:07:14.650
has already been reduced by 20%.

610
01:07:14.730 --> 01:07:18.860
The forest gives way
to cattle ranches or soybean farms.

611
01:07:18.950 --> 01:07:21.120
Ninety-five percent
of these soybeans...

612
01:07:21.200 --> 01:07:26.120
are used to feed livestock
and poultry in Europe and Asia.

613
01:07:35.460 --> 01:07:39.930
And so, a forest is turned into meat.

614
01:07:47.980 --> 01:07:50.520
When they burn,
forests and their soils...

615
01:07:50.600 --> 01:07:52.900
release huge quantities of carbon,

616
01:07:52.980 --> 01:07:58.570
accounting for 20% of the
greenhouse gases emitted across the globe.

617
01:07:58.650 --> 01:08:03.070
Deforestation is one of the principal
causes of global warming.

618
01:08:13.790 --> 01:08:17.710
Thousands of species
disappear forever.

619
01:08:17.800 --> 01:08:22.970
With them, one of the links
in a long chain of evolution snaps.

620
01:08:23.050 --> 01:08:26.390
The intelligence of the living matter
from which they came...

621
01:08:26.470 --> 01:08:28.470
is lost forever.

622
01:08:44.410 --> 01:08:48.580
Barely 20 years ago, Borneo,
the fourth-largest island in the world,

623
01:08:48.660 --> 01:08:52.160
was covered by a vast primary forest.

624
01:08:52.250 --> 01:08:54.670
At the current rate of deforestation,

625
01:08:54.750 --> 01:08:58.210
it will have totally
disappeared within 10 years.

626
01:08:58.300 --> 01:09:04.130
Living matter bonds water,
air, earth and the sun.

627
01:09:04.220 --> 01:09:07.050
In Borneo,
this bond has been broken...

628
01:09:07.140 --> 01:09:11.430
in what was one of the Earth's
greatest reservoirs of biodiversity.

629
01:09:26.570 --> 01:09:29.160
This catastrophe was
provoked by the decision...

630
01:09:29.240 --> 01:09:31.290
to produce palm oil,

631
01:09:31.370 --> 01:09:35.000
the most consumed oil
in the world, on Borneo.

632
01:09:35.080 --> 01:09:39.210
Palm oil not only caters
to our growing demand for food,

633
01:09:39.300 --> 01:09:44.760
but also cosmetics, detergents
and, increasingly, alternative fuels.

634
01:09:48.760 --> 01:09:53.890
The forest diversity was replaced
by a single species- the oil palm.

635
01:09:53.980 --> 01:09:57.860
Mono culture is easy,
productive and rapid.

636
01:09:57.940 --> 01:10:00.900
For local people,
it provides employment.

637
01:10:00.980 --> 01:10:03.440
It is an agricultural industry.

638
01:10:12.450 --> 01:10:16.920
Another example of massive
deforestation is the eucalyptus.

639
01:10:17.000 --> 01:10:19.750
Eucalyptus is used to make paper pulp.

640
01:10:19.840 --> 01:10:25.510
Plantations are growing, as demand
for paper has increased fivefold in 50 years.

641
01:10:31.470 --> 01:10:36.100
Mono cultures of trees
are gaining ground all over the world.

642
01:10:36.190 --> 01:10:39.860
But a monoculture is not a forest.

643
01:10:39.940 --> 01:10:44.030
By definition,
there is little diversity.

644
01:10:44.110 --> 01:10:48.070
One forest does not
replace another forest.

645
01:10:48.160 --> 01:10:50.070
At the foot of these eucalyptus trees,

646
01:10:50.160 --> 01:10:53.790
nothing grows,
because their leaves form a bed...

647
01:10:53.870 --> 01:10:56.790
that is toxic for most other plants.

648
01:10:56.870 --> 01:11:01.340
They grow quickly,
but exhaust water reserves.

649
01:11:17.100 --> 01:11:21.520
Soybeans, palm oil, eucalyptus trees-

650
01:11:21.610 --> 01:11:26.820
Deforestation destroys the essential
to produce the superfluous.

651
01:11:26.900 --> 01:11:31.370
But elsewhere, deforestation
is a last resort to survive.

652
01:11:36.250 --> 01:11:38.620
Over two billion people-

653
01:11:38.710 --> 01:11:41.630
almost a third
of the world's population—

654
01:11:41.710 --> 01:11:43.630
still depend on charcoal.

655
01:11:55.220 --> 01:11:59.020
In Haiti, one of the world's
poorest countries,

656
01:11:59.100 --> 01:12:03.480
charcoal is one of
the population's main consumables.

657
01:12:03.560 --> 01:12:06.530
Once the pearl of the Caribbean,

658
01:12:06.610 --> 01:12:10.150
Haiti can no longer feed
its population without foreign aid.

659
01:12:24.340 --> 01:12:28.840
On the hills of Haiti, only
two percent of the forests are left.

660
01:12:28.920 --> 01:12:32.930
Stripped bare, the soil
no longer absorbs the rainwater.

661
01:12:33.010 --> 01:12:36.510
With no vegetation
and no roots to reinforce them,

662
01:12:36.600 --> 01:12:39.270
nothing holds the soils back.

663
01:12:39.350 --> 01:12:43.600
The rainwater washes them
down the hillsides as far as the sea.

664
01:12:43.690 --> 01:12:46.900
Erosion impoverishes
the quality of the soils,

665
01:12:46.980 --> 01:12:49.990
reducing their suitability
for agriculture.

666
01:12:52.360 --> 01:12:57.120
In some parts of Madagascar,
the erosion is spectacular.

667
01:12:57.200 --> 01:13:01.660
Whole hillsides bear deep gashes
hundreds of meters wide.

668
01:13:10.800 --> 01:13:15.050
Thin and fragile,
soil is made by living matter.

669
01:13:15.140 --> 01:13:17.890
With erosion, the fine layer of humus,

670
01:13:17.970 --> 01:13:21.980
which took thousands of years
to form, disappears.

671
01:14:07.400 --> 01:14:10.570
Here's one theory
of the story of the Rapa Nui,

672
01:14:10.650 --> 01:14:12.730
the inhabitants of Easter Island,

673
01:14:12.820 --> 01:14:15.320
that could perhaps
give us pause for thought.

674
01:14:16.660 --> 01:14:19.780
Living on the most isolated
island in the world,

675
01:14:19.870 --> 01:14:22.410
the Rapa Nui exploited
their resources...

676
01:14:22.500 --> 01:14:24.750
until there was nothing left.

677
01:14:24.830 --> 01:14:28.710
Their civilization did not survive.

678
01:14:28.790 --> 01:14:32.590
On these lands stood
the highest palm trees in the world.

679
01:14:32.670 --> 01:14:34.630
They have disappeared.

680
01:14:34.720 --> 01:14:37.630
The Rapa Nui chopped them
all down for lumber.

681
01:14:37.720 --> 01:14:40.550
They then had to face
widespread soil erosion.

682
01:14:41.890 --> 01:14:44.270
The Rapa Nui could
no longer go fishing.

683
01:14:44.350 --> 01:14:47.600
There were no trees to build canoes.

684
01:14:47.690 --> 01:14:52.610
And yet the Rapa Nui formed one of
the most brilliant civilizations in the Pacific.

685
01:14:52.690 --> 01:14:56.780
Innovative farmers, sculptors,
exceptional navigators,

686
01:14:56.860 --> 01:15:01.280
they were caught in a vise of
overpopulation and dwindling resources.

687
01:15:04.290 --> 01:15:08.830
They experienced social unrest,
revolts and famine.

688
01:15:08.920 --> 01:15:11.630
Many did not survive the cataclysm.

689
01:16:00.680 --> 01:16:05.640
The real mystery of Easter Island
is not how its strange statues got there.

690
01:16:05.720 --> 01:16:07.640
We know now.

691
01:16:07.730 --> 01:16:11.100
It's why the Rapa Nui
didn't react in time.

692
01:16:17.990 --> 01:16:20.610
It's only one of a number of theories,

693
01:16:20.700 --> 01:16:23.320
but it has particular
relevance to us today.

694
01:16:24.950 --> 01:16:30.000
Since 1950, the world's
population has almost tripled.

695
01:16:30.080 --> 01:16:35.420
And since 1950, we have more fundamentally
altered our island, the Earth,

696
01:16:35.500 --> 01:16:39.670
than in all of our
200,000-year history.

697
01:16:43.720 --> 01:16:47.600
Nigeria is the biggest
oil exporter in Africa,

698
01:16:47.680 --> 01:16:52.730
and yet 70% of the population
lives under the poverty line.

699
01:16:52.810 --> 01:16:58.320
The wealth is there, but the country's
inhabitants don't have access to it.

700
01:16:58.400 --> 01:17:00.900
The same is true all over the globe.

701
01:17:00.990 --> 01:17:05.200
Half the world's poor
live in resource-rich countries.

702
01:17:21.550 --> 01:17:25.890
Our mode of development
has not fulfilled its promises.

703
01:17:25.970 --> 01:17:29.640
In 50 years, the gap
between rich and poor...

704
01:17:29.720 --> 01:17:32.390
has grown wider than ever.

705
01:17:32.480 --> 01:17:35.060
Today, half the world's wealth...

706
01:17:35.150 --> 01:17:39.820
is in the hands of the richest
two percent of the population.

707
01:17:43.490 --> 01:17:45.660
Can such disparities be maintained?

708
01:17:46.820 --> 01:17:48.910
They're the cause
of population movements...

709
01:17:48.990 --> 01:17:52.660
whose scale we have yet
to fully realize.

710
01:17:52.750 --> 01:17:58.420
The city of Lagos had a population
of 700,000 in 1960.

711
01:17:58.500 --> 01:18:02.760
That will rise to 16 million by 2025.

712
01:18:07.760 --> 01:18:11.850
Lagos is one of the fastest-growing
megalopolises in the world.

713
01:18:11.930 --> 01:18:15.520
The new arrivals are mostly
farmers forced off the land...

714
01:18:15.600 --> 01:18:18.190
for economic or demographic reasons,

715
01:18:18.270 --> 01:18:21.070
or because of diminishing resources.

716
01:18:27.240 --> 01:18:29.620
This is a radically new
type of urban growth,

717
01:18:29.700 --> 01:18:34.120
driven by the urge to survive
rather than to prosper.

718
01:18:37.630 --> 01:18:40.460
Every week, over a million people...

719
01:18:40.540 --> 01:18:43.710
swell the populations
of the world's cities.

720
01:19:01.270 --> 01:19:04.530
One human being in six
now lives in a precarious,

721
01:19:04.610 --> 01:19:07.200
unhealthy, overpopulated environment,

722
01:19:07.280 --> 01:19:09.360
without access to daily necessities...

723
01:19:09.450 --> 01:19:12.620
such as water,
sanitation or electricity.

724
01:19:44.070 --> 01:19:46.230
Hunger is spreading once more.

725
01:19:46.320 --> 01:19:48.610
It affects nearly one billion people.

726
01:20:33.870 --> 01:20:38.240
All over the planet, the poorest
scrabble to survive on scraps,

727
01:20:38.330 --> 01:20:42.210
while we continue to dig for resources
that we can no longer live without.

728
01:20:43.710 --> 01:20:45.790
We look farther and farther afield,

729
01:20:45.880 --> 01:20:48.420
in previously unspoilt territory...

730
01:20:48.510 --> 01:20:52.720
and in regions that are
increasingly difficult to exploit.

731
01:21:01.770 --> 01:21:03.730
We're not changing our model.

732
01:21:04.900 --> 01:21:07.570
Oil might run out?

733
01:21:07.650 --> 01:21:12.280
We can still extract oil
from the tar sands of Canada.

734
01:21:12.360 --> 01:21:16.570
The biggest trucks in the world
move thousands of tons of sand.

735
01:21:16.660 --> 01:21:19.120
The process of heating
and separating bitumen...

736
01:21:19.200 --> 01:21:23.330
from the sand requires
millions of cubic meters of water.

737
01:21:24.790 --> 01:21:27.750
Colossal amounts of energy are needed.

738
01:21:27.840 --> 01:21:30.590
The pollution is catastrophic.

739
01:21:32.840 --> 01:21:35.130
The most urgent priority, apparently,

740
01:21:35.220 --> 01:21:38.300
is to pick every pocket of sunlight.

741
01:22:13.670 --> 01:22:16.840
Our oil tankers are getting
bigger and bigger.

742
01:22:16.930 --> 01:22:20.050
Our energy requirements
are constantly increasing.

743
01:22:20.140 --> 01:22:23.310
We try to power growth
like a bottomless oven...

744
01:22:23.390 --> 01:22:26.140
that demands more and more fuel.

745
01:22:43.040 --> 01:22:44.950
It's all about carbon.

746
01:22:45.040 --> 01:22:48.960
In a few decades, the carbon
that made our atmosphere a furnace,

747
01:22:49.040 --> 01:22:52.170
and that nature captured
over millions of years,

748
01:22:52.250 --> 01:22:54.170
allowing life to develop,

749
01:22:54.260 --> 01:22:56.880
will have largely been
pumped back out.

750
01:22:56.970 --> 01:22:59.510
The atmosphere is heating up.

751
01:23:01.430 --> 01:23:05.890
It would have been inconceivable
for a boat to be here just a few years ago.

752
01:23:09.310 --> 01:23:14.070
Transport, industry,
deforestation, agriculture-

753
01:23:14.150 --> 01:23:18.860
Our activities release gigantic
quantities of carbon dioxide.

754
01:23:18.950 --> 01:23:22.490
Without realizing it,
molecule by molecule,

755
01:23:22.580 --> 01:23:26.080
we have upset the Earth's
climatic balance.

756
01:23:31.130 --> 01:23:33.130
All eyes are on the poles,

757
01:23:34.420 --> 01:23:38.510
where the effects of
global warming are most visible.

758
01:23:38.590 --> 01:23:42.930
It's happening fast- very fast.

759
01:23:43.010 --> 01:23:46.600
The Northwest Passage that connects
America, Europe and Asia...

760
01:23:46.680 --> 01:23:49.190
via the pole is opening up.

761
01:23:49.270 --> 01:23:52.110
The Arctic ice cap is melting.

762
01:23:57.360 --> 01:23:59.530
Under the effect of global warming,

763
01:23:59.610 --> 01:24:04.870
the ice cap has lost 40%
of its thickness in 40 years.

764
01:24:04.950 --> 01:24:09.210
Its surface area in the summer
shrinks year by year.

765
01:24:09.290 --> 01:24:12.540
It could disappear before 2030.

766
01:24:12.630 --> 01:24:15.880
Some predictions suggest 2015.

767
01:24:15.960 --> 01:24:20.470
Soon these waters will be free of ice
several summer months a year.

768
01:24:35.650 --> 01:24:39.240
The sunbeams that the ice sheet
previously reflected back...

769
01:24:39.320 --> 01:24:42.410
now penetrate the dark water,
heating it up.

770
01:24:42.490 --> 01:24:45.320
The warming process gathers pace.

771
01:24:54.420 --> 01:24:58.340
This ice contains
the records of our planet.

772
01:24:58.420 --> 01:25:00.840
The concentration of carbon dioxide...

773
01:25:00.920 --> 01:25:04.890
hasn't been so high
for several hundred thousand years.

774
01:25:06.180 --> 01:25:10.640
Humanity has never lived
in an atmosphere like this.

775
01:25:21.990 --> 01:25:27.700
Is excessive exploitation of our
resources threatening the lives of every species?

776
01:25:27.790 --> 01:25:30.950
Climate change accentuates the threat.

777
01:25:31.040 --> 01:25:34.750
By 2050, a quarter
of the Earth's species...

778
01:25:34.830 --> 01:25:38.420
could be threatened with extinction.

779
01:25:38.500 --> 01:25:43.670
In these polar regions, the balance of nature
has already been disrupted.

780
01:27:29.660 --> 01:27:33.740
Off the coast of Greenland,
there are more and more icebergs.

781
01:28:00.400 --> 01:28:02.860
Around the North Pole,
the ice cap has lost...

782
01:28:02.940 --> 01:28:06.780
30% of its surface area in 30 years.

783
01:28:09.860 --> 01:28:12.370
But as Greenland
rapidly becomes warmer,

784
01:28:12.450 --> 01:28:14.620
the freshwater of a whole continent...

785
01:28:14.700 --> 01:28:18.910
flows into the salt water
of the oceans.

786
01:28:19.000 --> 01:28:24.210
Greenland's ice contains 20%
of the freshwater of the whole planet.

787
01:28:24.290 --> 01:28:28.800
If it melts, sea levels will
rise by nearly seven meters.

788
01:29:02.080 --> 01:29:04.420
But there is no industry here.

789
01:29:05.750 --> 01:29:08.500
Greenland's ice sheet
suffers from greenhouse gases...

790
01:29:08.590 --> 01:29:11.420
emitted elsewhere on Earth.

791
01:29:11.510 --> 01:29:14.510
Our ecosystem doesn't have borders.

792
01:29:14.590 --> 01:29:19.520
Wherever we are, our actions
have repercussions on the whole Earth.

793
01:29:19.600 --> 01:29:23.520
The atmosphere of our planet
is an indivisible whole.

794
01:29:23.600 --> 01:29:26.440
It is an asset we share.

795
01:29:26.520 --> 01:29:30.900
On Greenland's surface,
lakes are appearing on the landscape.

796
01:29:30.990 --> 01:29:33.200
The ice cap has begun
to melt at a speed...

797
01:29:33.280 --> 01:29:38.120
that even the most pessimistic scientists
did not envision 10 years ago.

798
01:29:47.590 --> 01:29:50.550
More and more
of these glacier-fed rivers...

799
01:29:50.630 --> 01:29:54.590
are merging together
and burrowing through the surface.

800
01:29:54.680 --> 01:29:58.720
It was thought the water would freeze
in the depths of the ice.

801
01:29:58.810 --> 01:30:01.850
On the contrary,
it flows under the ice,

802
01:30:01.930 --> 01:30:03.890
carrying the ice sheet into the sea,

803
01:30:03.980 --> 01:30:05.940
where it breaks into icebergs.

804
01:30:48.690 --> 01:30:50.940
As the freshwater
of Greenland's ice sheet...

805
01:30:51.020 --> 01:30:54.110
gradually seeps into
the salt water of the oceans,

806
01:30:54.190 --> 01:30:57.860
low-lying lands
around the globe are threatened.

807
01:31:07.120 --> 01:31:09.500
Sea levels are rising.

808
01:31:09.580 --> 01:31:12.590
Water expanding
as it gets warmer caused,

809
01:31:12.670 --> 01:31:17.130
in the 20th century alone,
a rise of 20 centimeters.

810
01:31:17.220 --> 01:31:20.430
Everything becomes unstable.

811
01:31:20.510 --> 01:31:24.100
Coral reefs, for example,
are extremely sensitive...

812
01:31:24.180 --> 01:31:26.600
to the slightest change
in water temperature.

813
01:31:26.690 --> 01:31:29.100
Thirty percent have disappeared.

814
01:31:29.190 --> 01:31:32.360
They are an essential link
in the chain of species.

815
01:31:47.830 --> 01:31:52.460
In the atmosphere, the major wind
streams are changing direction.

816
01:31:52.540 --> 01:31:54.840
Rain cycles are altered.

817
01:31:54.920 --> 01:31:58.550
The geography of climates is modified.

818
01:31:58.630 --> 01:32:01.680
The inhabitants of low-lying
islands here in the Maldives,

819
01:32:01.760 --> 01:32:03.680
for example, are on the front line.

820
01:32:03.760 --> 01:32:06.220
They are increasingly concerned.

821
01:32:06.310 --> 01:32:09.230
Some are already looking
for new, more hospitable lands.

822
01:32:16.280 --> 01:32:20.200
If sea levels continue
to rise faster and faster,

823
01:32:20.280 --> 01:32:22.410
what will major cities like Tokyo,

824
01:32:22.490 --> 01:32:25.580
the world's most populous city, do?

825
01:32:25.660 --> 01:32:29.710
Every year, scientists' predictions
become more and more alarming.

826
01:32:29.790 --> 01:32:34.920
Seventy percent of the world's
population lives on coastal plains.

827
01:32:35.000 --> 01:32:41.180
Eleven of the 15 biggest cities
stand on a coastline or river estuary.

828
01:32:41.260 --> 01:32:44.390
As the seas rise,
salt will invade the water table,

829
01:32:44.470 --> 01:32:47.640
depriving inhabitants
of drinking water.

830
01:33:02.320 --> 01:33:04.910
Migratory phenomena are inevitable.

831
01:33:04.990 --> 01:33:08.330
The only uncertainty
concerns their scale.

832
01:33:36.400 --> 01:33:41.150
In Africa, Mount Kilimanjaro
is unrecognizable.

833
01:33:41.240 --> 01:33:44.150
Eighty percent of its
glaciers have disappeared.

834
01:33:44.240 --> 01:33:46.950
In summer, the rivers no longer flow.

835
01:33:47.030 --> 01:33:50.040
Local peoples are affected
by the lack of water.

836
01:33:51.710 --> 01:33:55.580
Even on the world's highest peaks,
in the heart of the Himalayas,

837
01:33:55.670 --> 01:33:58.290
eternal snows and glaciers
are receding.

838
01:34:02.470 --> 01:34:06.340
Yet these glaciers play
an essential role in the water cycle.

839
01:34:06.430 --> 01:34:09.350
They trap the water
from the monsoons as ice...

840
01:34:09.430 --> 01:34:12.600
and release it in the summer
when the snow melts.

841
01:34:23.990 --> 01:34:28.410
The glaciers of the Himalayas are the source
of all the great Asian rivers-

842
01:34:28.490 --> 01:34:32.750
the Indus, Ganges,
Mekong, Yangtze Kiang.

843
01:34:32.830 --> 01:34:36.750
Two billion people
depend on them for drinking water...

844
01:34:36.830 --> 01:34:40.460
and to irrigate their crops,
as in Bangladesh.

845
01:34:45.550 --> 01:34:48.840
On the delta of the Ganges
and Brahmaputra,

846
01:34:48.930 --> 01:34:52.060
Bangladesh is directly
affected by the phenomena...

847
01:34:52.140 --> 01:34:55.270
occurring in the Himalayas
and at sea level.

848
01:34:55.350 --> 01:34:59.270
This is one of the most populous
and poorest countries in the world.

849
01:34:59.360 --> 01:35:01.570
It is already hit by global warming.

850
01:35:01.650 --> 01:35:06.490
The combined impact of increasingly
dramatic floods and hurricanes...

851
01:35:06.570 --> 01:35:09.410
could make a third
of its landmass disappear.

852
01:35:09.490 --> 01:35:13.080
When populations are subjected
to these devastating phenomena,

853
01:35:13.160 --> 01:35:15.450
they eventually move away.

854
01:35:22.130 --> 01:35:24.460
Wealthy countries will not be spared.

855
01:35:24.550 --> 01:35:27.550
Droughts are occurring
all over the planet.

856
01:35:27.630 --> 01:35:31.970
In Australia,
half of farmland is already affected.

857
01:36:17.060 --> 01:36:21.230
We are in the process
of compromising the climatic balance...

858
01:36:21.310 --> 01:36:25.650
that has allowed us
to develop over 12,000 years.

859
01:36:35.540 --> 01:36:39.750
More and more wildfires
encroach on major cities.

860
01:36:39.830 --> 01:36:43.250
In turn, they exacerbate
global warming.

861
01:36:43.330 --> 01:36:47.550
As the trees burn,
they release carbon dioxide.

862
01:36:47.630 --> 01:36:52.470
The system that controls our climate
has been severely disrupted.

863
01:36:52.550 --> 01:36:56.720
The elements on which it relies
have been disrupted.

864
01:37:52.450 --> 01:37:57.320
The clock of climate change is ticking
in these magnificent landscapes.

865
01:37:58.790 --> 01:38:02.450
Here in Siberia
and elsewhere across the globe,

866
01:38:02.540 --> 01:38:06.750
it is so cold that the ground
is constantly frozen.

867
01:38:06.830 --> 01:38:09.170
It's known as permafrost.

868
01:38:23.270 --> 01:38:27.900
Under its surface lies
a climatic time bomb: methane,

869
01:38:27.980 --> 01:38:33.240
a greenhouse gas 20 times
more powerful than carbon dioxide.

870
01:38:33.320 --> 01:38:36.030
If the permafrost melts,
the methane released...

871
01:38:36.110 --> 01:38:39.830
would cause the greenhouse effect
to race out of control,

872
01:38:39.910 --> 01:38:42.740
with consequences no one can predict.

873
01:38:42.830 --> 01:38:46.670
We would literally be
in unknown territory.

874
01:39:09.060 --> 01:39:13.110
Humanity has no more than 10 years
to reverse the trend...

875
01:39:13.190 --> 01:39:16.190
and avoid crossing
into this territory—

876
01:39:16.280 --> 01:39:18.610
life on Earth
as we have never known it.

877
01:39:51.230 --> 01:39:54.980
We have created phenomena
we cannot control.

878
01:39:55.070 --> 01:40:01.410
Since our origins, water, air
and forms of life are intimately linked.

879
01:40:01.490 --> 01:40:05.830
But recently,
we have broken those links.

880
01:40:05.910 --> 01:40:08.410
Let's face the facts.

881
01:40:08.500 --> 01:40:11.580
We must believe what we know.

882
01:40:11.670 --> 01:40:16.840
All that we have just seen
is a reflection of human behavior.

883
01:40:16.920 --> 01:40:20.760
We have shaped the Earth in our image.

884
01:40:20.840 --> 01:40:24.180
We have very little time to change.

885
01:40:24.260 --> 01:40:28.770
How can this century carry the burden
of nine billion human beings...

886
01:40:28.850 --> 01:40:31.770
if we refuse to be called
to account...

887
01:40:31.850 --> 01:40:34.690
for everything we alone have done?

888
01:44:31.340 --> 01:44:33.600
The cost of our actions is high.

889
01:44:33.680 --> 01:44:37.430
Others pay the price without
having been actively involved.

890
01:44:37.520 --> 01:44:40.730
I have seen refugee camps
as big as cities,

891
01:44:40.810 --> 01:44:42.940
sprawling in the desert.

892
01:44:43.020 --> 01:44:45.730
How many men, women and children...

893
01:44:45.820 --> 01:44:47.780
will be left by the wayside tomorrow?

894
01:44:51.700 --> 01:44:53.620
Must we always build walls...

895
01:44:53.700 --> 01:44:55.780
to break the chain
of human solidarity,

896
01:44:55.870 --> 01:45:01.790
separate peoples and protect the
happiness of some from the misery of others?

897
01:45:01.870 --> 01:45:04.500
It's too late to be a pessimist.

898
01:45:04.590 --> 01:45:08.210
I know that a single human
can knock down every wall.

899
01:45:08.300 --> 01:45:10.880
It's too late to be a pessimist.

900
01:45:10.970 --> 01:45:15.220
Worldwide, four children
out of five attend school.

901
01:45:15.300 --> 01:45:18.470
Never has learning been given
to so many human beings.

902
01:45:18.560 --> 01:45:23.060
Everyone, from richest to poorest,
can make a contribution.

903
01:45:23.150 --> 01:45:25.900
Lesotho, one of the world's
poorest countries,

904
01:45:25.980 --> 01:45:30.740
is proportionally the one that invests
most in its people's education.

905
01:45:30.820 --> 01:45:33.410
Qatar, one of the world's
richest states,

906
01:45:33.490 --> 01:45:36.410
has opened its doors
to the best universities.

907
01:45:36.490 --> 01:45:42.250
Culture, education, research and innovation
are inexhaustible resources.

908
01:45:42.330 --> 01:45:45.040
In the face of misery and suffering,

909
01:45:45.130 --> 01:45:48.750
millions of N.G.O.'s prove
that solidarity between peoples...

910
01:45:48.840 --> 01:45:52.130
is stronger than
the selfishness of nations.

911
01:45:52.220 --> 01:45:55.300
In Bangladesh, a man
thought the unthinkable...

912
01:45:55.390 --> 01:45:58.430
and founded a bank
that lends only to the poor.

913
01:45:58.510 --> 01:46:01.270
In barely 30 years,
it has changed the lives...

914
01:46:01.350 --> 01:46:04.980
of 150 million people
around the world.

915
01:46:06.940 --> 01:46:10.530
Antarctica is a continent
with immense natural resources...

916
01:46:10.610 --> 01:46:12.940
that no country can claim for itself,

917
01:46:13.030 --> 01:46:16.700
a natural reserve devoted
to peace and science.

918
01:46:16.780 --> 01:46:21.750
A treaty signed by 49 states has made
it a treasureshared by all humanity.

919
01:46:22.960 --> 01:46:24.870
It's too late to be a pessimist.

920
01:46:24.960 --> 01:46:29.790
Governments have acted to protect nearly
two percent of the world's territorial waters.

921
01:46:29.880 --> 01:46:32.840
It's not much, but it's two times
more than 10 years ago.

922
01:46:32.920 --> 01:46:37.340
The first natural parks were created
just over a century ago.

923
01:46:37.430 --> 01:46:40.560
They cover over 13% of the continents.

924
01:46:40.640 --> 01:46:43.310
They create spaces where
human activity is in step...

925
01:46:43.390 --> 01:46:47.560
with the preservation
of species, soils and landscapes.

926
01:46:47.650 --> 01:46:52.070
This harmony between humans
and nature can become the rule,

927
01:46:52.150 --> 01:46:54.070
no longer the exception.

928
01:46:54.150 --> 01:46:58.240
In the United States, New York has
realized that nature does for us.

929
01:46:58.320 --> 01:47:02.330
These forests and lakes supply
all the drinking water the city needs.

930
01:47:07.170 --> 01:47:11.210
In South Korea, the forests
have been devastated by war.

931
01:47:11.300 --> 01:47:14.210
Thanks to a national
reforestation program,

932
01:47:14.300 --> 01:47:17.380
they once more
cover 65% of the country.

933
01:47:17.470 --> 01:47:21.600
More than 75% of paper is recycled.

934
01:47:21.680 --> 01:47:26.980
Costa Rica has made a choice between military
spending and the conservation of its lands.

935
01:47:27.060 --> 01:47:28.980
The country no longer has an army.

936
01:47:29.060 --> 01:47:32.400
It prefers to devote its resources
to education, ecotourism...

937
01:47:32.480 --> 01:47:35.400
and the protection
of its primary forest.

938
01:47:35.490 --> 01:47:38.570
Gabon is one of the world's
leading producers of wood.

939
01:47:38.660 --> 01:47:40.910
It enforces selective logging—

940
01:47:40.990 --> 01:47:43.580
not more than one tree every hectare.

941
01:47:43.660 --> 01:47:47.250
Its forests are one of the country's
most important economic resources,

942
01:47:47.330 --> 01:47:50.130
but they have the time to regenerate.

943
01:47:50.210 --> 01:47:54.590
Programs exist that guarantee
sustainable forest management.

944
01:47:54.670 --> 01:47:57.010
They must become mandatory.

945
01:48:01.010 --> 01:48:03.100
For consumers and producers,

946
01:48:03.180 --> 01:48:05.850
justice is an opportunity
to be seized.

947
01:48:05.930 --> 01:48:09.600
When trade is fair,
when both buyer and seller benefit,

948
01:48:09.690 --> 01:48:12.560
everybody can prosper
and earn a decent living.

949
01:48:20.030 --> 01:48:22.070
How can there be justice and equity...

950
01:48:22.160 --> 01:48:25.160
between people whose only tools
are their hands...

951
01:48:25.240 --> 01:48:29.910
and those who harvest their crops
with a machine and state subsidies?

952
01:48:36.420 --> 01:48:38.880
Let's be responsible consumers.

953
01:48:38.970 --> 01:48:41.130
Think about what we buy.

954
01:48:42.970 --> 01:48:45.720
It's too late to be a pessimist.

955
01:48:45.810 --> 01:48:48.140
I have seen agriculture
on a human scale.

956
01:48:48.230 --> 01:48:53.270
It can feed the whole planet if meat production
doesn't take the food out of people's mouths.

957
01:48:53.360 --> 01:48:57.360
I have seen fishermen
who take care what they catch...

958
01:48:57.440 --> 01:48:59.900
and care for the riches of the ocean.

959
01:49:01.110 --> 01:49:04.030
I have seen houses
producing their own energy.

960
01:49:04.120 --> 01:49:08.200
5,000 people live in the world's
first ever eco-friendly district,

961
01:49:08.290 --> 01:49:10.210
in Freiburg, Germany.

962
01:49:10.290 --> 01:49:12.250
Other cities partner the project.

963
01:49:12.330 --> 01:49:15.500
Mumbai is the thousandth to join them.

964
01:49:18.460 --> 01:49:22.380
The governments of New Zealand,
Iceland, Austria, Sweden...

965
01:49:22.470 --> 01:49:24.720
and other nations
have made the development...

966
01:49:24.800 --> 01:49:28.100
of renewable energy sources
a top priority.

967
01:49:28.180 --> 01:49:31.890
I know that 80% of the energy
we consume...

968
01:49:31.980 --> 01:49:34.230
comes from fossil energy sources.

969
01:49:34.310 --> 01:49:39.570
Every week, two new coal-fired generating
plants are built in China alone.

970
01:49:39.650 --> 01:49:42.240
But I have also seen, in Denmark,

971
01:49:42.320 --> 01:49:44.910
a prototype of a coal-fired plant...

972
01:49:44.990 --> 01:49:48.740
that releases its carbon
into the soil rather than the air.

973
01:49:48.830 --> 01:49:51.450
A solution for the future?
Nobody knows yet.

974
01:49:52.670 --> 01:49:54.580
I have seen, in Iceland,

975
01:49:54.670 --> 01:49:59.460
an electricity plant powered
by the Earth's heat— geothermal power.

976
01:49:59.550 --> 01:50:02.590
I have seen a sea snake
lying on the swell...

977
01:50:02.680 --> 01:50:06.840
to absorb the energy of the waves
and produce electricity.

978
01:50:11.020 --> 01:50:13.980
I have seen wind farms
off the coast of Denmark...

979
01:50:14.060 --> 01:50:17.770
that produce 20%
of the country's electricity.

980
01:50:17.860 --> 01:50:21.820
The U.S.A., China, India,
Germany and Spain...

981
01:50:21.900 --> 01:50:25.110
are the biggest investors
in renewable energy.

982
01:50:25.200 --> 01:50:29.030
They have already created over
two and a half million jobs.

983
01:50:29.120 --> 01:50:32.540
Where on Earth doesn't the wind blow?

984
01:50:34.080 --> 01:50:39.460
I have seen desert expanses
baking in the sun.

985
01:50:39.550 --> 01:50:41.630
Everything on Earth is linked,

986
01:50:41.710 --> 01:50:46.130
and the Earth is linked to the sun,
its original energy source.

987
01:50:46.220 --> 01:50:50.010
Can humans not imitate plants
and capture its energy?

988
01:50:50.100 --> 01:50:53.180
In one hour,
the sun gives the Earth...

989
01:50:53.270 --> 01:50:58.810
the same amount of energy as that
consumed by all humanity in one year.

990
01:50:58.900 --> 01:51:03.740
As long as the Earth exists,
the sun's energy will be inexhaustible.

991
01:51:03.820 --> 01:51:08.320
All we have to do is stop drilling
the Earth and start looking to the sky.

992
01:51:08.410 --> 01:51:11.740
All we have to do
is learn to cultivate the sun.

993
01:51:11.830 --> 01:51:17.370
All these experiments are only examples,
but they testify to a new awareness.

994
01:51:17.460 --> 01:51:20.920
They lay down markers
for a new human adventure...

995
01:51:21.000 --> 01:51:25.260
based on moderation,
intelligence and sharing.

996
01:51:41.020 --> 01:51:43.780
It's time to come together.

997
01:51:43.860 --> 01:51:49.280
What's important is not what's gone,
but what remains.

998
01:51:49.370 --> 01:51:52.280
We still have
half the world's forests,

999
01:51:52.370 --> 01:51:54.950
thousands of rivers,
lakes and glaciers...

1000
01:51:55.040 --> 01:51:57.120
and thousands of thriving species.

1001
01:52:01.750 --> 01:52:05.550
We know that the solutions
are there today.

1002
01:52:05.630 --> 01:52:08.130
We all have the power to change.

1003
01:52:10.140 --> 01:52:12.800
So what are we waiting for?
